 # Densification Strategy
 
-Also called: Gaussian densification, primitive management, adaptive density control
+Also called: Gaussian Densification, Adaptive Density Control, Primitive Management
 
 ## Overview
-Densification strategies manage the topology of the 3D Gaussian primitive set $\mathcal{G}$ during the training of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by dynamically adding, splitting, cloning, or removing primitives [Parent]. This family addresses the problem of under-representing complex scene details or over-representing empty space, which static initialization from sparse Structure-from-Motion (SfM) points cannot resolve [Parent]. What sets this family apart from static initialization or fixed-parameter methods is the iterative, feedback-driven adaptation of the primitive population throughout the training process, balancing reconstruction quality against computational cost [Parent].
+Densification Strategy defines the mechanisms for dynamically managing the number and spatial distribution of 3D Gaussian primitives during the training of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) [Parent]. It addresses the problem of under-representing complex scene details or over-representing empty space by splitting, cloning, or pruning Gaussians based on optimization metrics [Parent]. What sets this family apart from static initialization methods is the iterative, feedback-driven adaptation of the primitive set throughout the training process, balancing reconstruction quality against computational cost [Parent].
 
