 ### [VS-Splat Voxel Selection](9132-vs-splat-voxel-selection.md)
-VS-Splat Voxel Selection is a learnable, differentiable mechanism for selecting relevant 3D Gaussian primitives from a dense voxel grid during the training of feed-forward Gaussian Splatting models. It solves the problem of inefficient primitive distribution by replacing static opacity thresholding with a neural network that predicts confidence scores for each voxel, allowing the model to focus computational resources on object-centric regions. It sets itself apart from its siblings by using a Gumbel-Sigmoid relaxation to enable gradient flow through the discrete selection process, unlike [Score-based Densification](4705-score-based-densification.md) which relies on 2D positional gradients, or [Straight-Through Estimator (STE) for Confidence Scores](9636-straight-through-estimator-ste-for-confidence-scores.md) which uses hard thresholding. See [VS-Splat Voxel Selection](9132-vs-splat-voxel-selection.md).
+VS-Splat Voxel Selection is a learnable, differentiable mechanism for selecting relevant 3D voxels in feed-forward Gaussian Splatting, replacing static opacity thresholding with a confidence-based selection process. It sets itself apart from siblings like [Opacity-Based Thresholding](6234-opacity-based-thresholding.md) by using a Gumbel-Sigmoid distribution to predict confidence scores, allowing the network to learn which voxels contain object-centric geometry rather than relying on manual hyperparameter tuning. See [VS-Splat Voxel Selection](9132-vs-splat-voxel-selection.md).
 
 ### [Adaptive Density Control](9188-adaptive-density-control.md)
-Adaptive Density Control (ADC) is a dynamic optimization strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that adjusts the number and distribution of Gaussian primitives during training to prevent under-reconstruction (missing geometry) and over-reconstruction (coarse approximations). It solves the problem of sparse initial Structure-from-Motion (SfM) point clouds by adding, cloning, or removing primitives based on gradient magnitude, scale, and opacity. What sets it apart from its siblings is its reliance on view-space positional gradient magnitudes accumulated over visible iterations to trigger deterministic splitting, cloning, or pruning, rather than stochastic sampling or importance-weighted reweighting. See [Adaptive Density Control](9188-adaptive-density-control.md).
+Adaptive Density Control (ADC) is a dynamic optimization strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) that adjusts the number and spatial distribution of Gaussian primitives during training to prevent under-reconstruction (missing geometry) and over-reconstruction (coarse approximations). It solves the problem of sparse initial Structure-from-Motion (SfM) point clouds by adding, cloning, or removing primitives based on gradient magnitude, scale, and opacity. What sets it apart from its siblings is its reliance on view-space positional gradient magnitudes accumulated over visible iterations to trigger deterministic splitting, cloning, or pruning, rather than stochastic sampling or importance-weighted reweighting. See [Adaptive Density Control](9188-adaptive-density-control.md).
 
 ### [Locality-preserving Densification](9585-locality-preserving-densification.md)
-Locality-preserving densification addresses the memory access throttling caused by standard Gaussian densification, where newly added primitives are appended to the end of parameter buffers, causing spatially close Gaussians to be stored far apart in memory. By reordering the Gaussian population based on 3D spatial proximity using Z-ordering (Morton order), it ensures that neighboring Gaussians in space are also adjacent in memory, thereby improving memory access coalescing. This method sets itself apart from sibling strategies like Score-based Densification by focusing on the memory layout and hardware efficiency rather than the geometric criteria for splitting or cloning primitives. See [Locality-preserving Densification](9585-locality-preserving-densification.md).
+Locality-preserving densification addresses the memory access throttling caused by standard Gaussian densification, where new primitives are appended to the end of parameter buffers, causing spatially close Gaussians to be stored far apart in memory. By reordering the Gaussian parameter buffers based on 3D spatial proximity using Z-ordering (Morton order), this method ensures that neighboring Gaussians in space are also adjacent in memory, thereby improving memory access coalescing. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [GroupFlow](6351-groupflow.md) or [3DGS-MCMC](7852-3dgs-mcmc.md) by focusing specifically on the data layout and memory hierarchy optimization rather than changing the geometric splitting logic or sampling distribution. See [Locality-preserving Densification](9585-locality-preserving-densification.md).
 
 ### [Progressive and Multi-stage Training](9966-progressive-and-multi-stage-training.md)
-Progressive and Multi-stage Training refers to a family of optimization strategies that decompose the 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) training process into sequential phases or stages, rather than optimizing the entire radiance field from a single initialization. This approach solves the problem of training instability, local minima, and insufficient initial coverage by decoupling the learning of global geometric structure from local appearance details or specific attributes. It sets itself apart from single-stage training by ensuring that the representation evolves from sparse/coarse to dense/fine in a controlled manner, preventing the optimizer from getting stuck in poor local minima caused by insufficient initial coverage or conflicting gradients. See [Progressive and Multi-stage Training](9966-progressive-and-multi-stage-training.md).
+Progressive and Multi-stage Training refers to strategies that refine a 3D Gaussian representation through iterative densification or distinct optimization phases, rather than optimizing a single static initialization. It solves the instability, poor convergence, and artifact generation associated with initializing dense point clouds or optimizing complex radiance fields from scratch. Compared to single-stage training, these methods yield higher rendering quality by progressively adding detail or decoupling difficult optimization tasks (such as depth or pose estimation) from the final rendering optimization. See [Progressive and Multi-stage Training](9966-progressive-and-multi-stage-training.md).
 
 ### [TangoGS](10180-tangogs.md)
-TangoGS is a density controller for 3D Gaussian Splatting that automatically scales the number of Gaussians across different capture sizes without per-capture retuning. It combines a capture-derived learning allowance (model sizing) with training-based adaptation (quality feedback) to determine the final Gaussian population. Unlike sibling methods that rely on fixed defaults or per-capture tuning, TangoGS ensures the model budget is invariant to redundant views by discounting track-adjusted pixels, while using reconstruction quality to dynamically adjust pruning thresholds. See [TangoGS](10180-tangogs.md).
+TangoGS is a density controller for 3D Gaussian Splatting that automatically scales the number of Gaussian primitives across different capture sizes without per-capture retuning. It combines a capture-derived learning allowance (model sizing) with training-based adaptation (quality feedback) to determine the target Gaussian population. Unlike sibling methods that rely on fixed defaults or per-capture tuning, TangoGS ensures the model budget is invariant to redundant views by using track-adjusted pixels normalized against standard scenes. See [TangoGS](10180-tangogs.md).
 
