 ## Background
-The concept of densification in 3D Gaussian Splatting originates from the original 3DGS paper [Parent], which introduced the idea of splitting and cloning Gaussians based on 2D positional gradients to handle under-reconstructed regions. Subsequent developments have expanded this into a diverse family of strategies addressing specific challenges such as dynamic scenes ([GroupFlow](6351-groupflow.md)), memory efficiency ([Locality-preserving Densification](9585-locality-preserving-densification.md)), and automated hyperparameter tuning ([TangoGS](10180-tangogs.md)). The field continues to evolve with methods integrating MCMC sampling ([3DGS-MCMC](7852-3dgs-mcmc.md)), neural refinement ([Adaptive Gaussian Refinement](11448-adaptive-gaussian-refinement.md)), and multi-stage training ([Progressive and Multi-stage Training](9966-progressive-and-multi-stage-training.md)).
+The concept of adaptive densification in 3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced in the original 3DGS paper [Parent], which established the baseline strategy of splitting and cloning Gaussians based on gradient magnitude and opacity thresholds. Subsequent research has expanded this family to address specific limitations such as dynamic scene modeling [GroupFlow](6351-groupflow.md), memory efficiency [Locality-preserving Densification](9585-locality-preserving-densification.md), and automated hyperparameter tuning [TangoGS](10180-tangogs.md). The field continues to evolve with methods integrating MCMC sampling [3DGS-MCMC](7852-3dgs-mcmc.md) and neural refinement [Adaptive Gaussian Refinement](11448-adaptive-gaussian-refinement.md).
 
